Title:
Qemouflage: Beyond the EDR’s Reach

Type:
Blog Post (with GitHub PoC Tool)

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
Qemouflage is a proof-of-concept initial-access payload that runs a fully provisioned Alpine Linux VM on a Windows host as a standard user, leveraging QEMU’s TCG (userspace CPU emulation) to avoid requiring admin rights or hardware virtualization. The core idea is that host EDRs instrument Windows primitives (processes, threads, ETW, minifilters, AMSI), but have no visibility into a guest OS whose processes and memory exist only inside QEMU’s address space. The dropper is a small standalone Windows EXE that downloads 7-Zip, extracts the QEMU NSIS installer without triggering UAC, fetches a stock Alpine cloud image, and provisions it at boot via cloud-init (NoCloud) served from a local HTTP endpoint. It also provides a “winexec” bridge: a host-side command server exposed into the guest via a reverse SSH tunnel, enabling Windows command execution from inside the Linux VM. This is useful for Red Teams and offensive security researchers exploring VM-based evasion and “tooling-in-guest” tradecraft under standard-user constraints. It’s interesting because it reframes “EDR bypass” as an observability gap: the EDR isn’t bypassed; it simply can’t introspect a userspace emulator’s guest state.

Technical Focus:
- QEMU TCG (Tiny Code Generator) userspace CPU emulation on Windows
- UAC-less deployment by extracting NSIS installers with 7-Zip
- Cloud-init NoCloud provisioning via SMBIOS serial + host-served HTTP seed
- QEMU user-mode networking (slirp) and host/guest connectivity patterns
- Host command execution bridge via reverse SSH tunneling + netcat wrapper
- PE/AV evasion knobs (resources, metadata, compiler variance) at a high level

Use Cases:
- Standard-user initial foothold that stages tooling inside a hidden Linux guest
- Running Linux-native post-exploitation tooling (Impacket, BloodHound.py, Nmap) with reduced host EDR telemetry
- Covert pivoting/tunneling from a guest environment (e.g., chisel/ligolo-ng) while host sees only QEMU traffic
- Researching detection opportunities around QEMU/TCG artifacts, slirp traffic, and unusual installer-extraction behavior
- Building autonomous “single EXE” tradecraft for controlled red team simulations

Keywords:
Qemouflage, QEMU, TCG, Tiny Code Generator, Alpine Linux, cloud-init, NoCloud datasource, SMBIOS serial, slirp, user-mode networking, NSIS extraction, 7-Zip, UAC bypass (no prompt), standard user execution, EDR visibility gap, ETW, AMSI, PsSetCreateProcessNotifyRoutine, ObRegisterCallbacks, reverse SSH tunnel, winexec, mingw-w64, WinHTTP, qcow2